# Files uploaded to EAS Build.
#
# ⚠️  THIS FILE *REPLACES* .gitignore — IT DOES NOT EXTEND IT. (The header used to say
# "mirrors .gitignore semantics", which cost us: it means the SYNTAX is gitignore's, not
# that .gitignore's ENTRIES still apply.) When .easignore exists, EAS CLI stops consulting
# .gitignore entirely, so anything gitignored but not listed here IS UPLOADED.
#
# The proof is three lines below: _media/, sim/ and qc/ are all in .gitignore, yet each had
# to be re-listed here to get the archive down from 439 MB. If .gitignore were still in
# effect, none of those entries would have been necessary.
#
# Consequence (security audit 2026-07-30, finding #1): .env — holding the LIVE Stripe secret
# key, the Cloudflare API token and the OpenAI key — was being uploaded to EAS Build on every
# single build. Not into the shipped IPA (only EXPO_PUBLIC_* is inlined there), but onto a
# third party's servers, retrievable by anyone with EAS project access or a stolen EAS token.
# The secrets block at the bottom of this file closes that. Add to it, never remove from it.
#
# DO NOT ignore: assets/ (holds proto.zip + app icons/splash), src/, ios-certs/
# (local build credentials), supabase/, proto/ (kept for safety), or any app config.

# Marketing website + its heavy PNG/WEBP/MP4 sources — a separate static site, not the app.
web/

# Screenshots, QA captures, audit + design artifacts.
_media/
qc/
sim/
docs/

# Claude Code tooling. The skills symlink farm can break the EAS tarball; nothing here
# is part of the build.
.claude/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- SCRATCH (2026-08-10)
# ~958 MB of gitignored scratch that was uploading on EVERY build, because — per the header —
# being in .gitignore does nothing here. Measured: .worktrees 530 MB / 14,855 files, .tmp 426 MB,
# the rest small. Two of these are not merely wasteful:
#
#   · .tmp/ is where tools drop generated cron keys (.gitignore calls them out by name:
#     "occasionally a secret"). `.tmp/billing-cron-key.local` was sitting there when this entry
#     was written. That is finding #1 of the 2026-07-30 audit recurring in a new directory — a
#     live credential shipped to a third party's servers on every build.
#   · .agents/skills/ is the SAME symlink farm .claude/ is excluded for four lines above. It was
#     never added, so the tarball hazard that entry exists to prevent was only half closed.
#
# .worktrees/ holds full checkouts of this repo on other branches. Uploading them means a build
# can carry a half-finished feature's source to EAS alongside the branch you meant to ship.
.worktrees/
.tmp/
.agents/
.superpowers/
.playwright-mcp/
__pycache__/
skills-lock.json
#
# Deliberately NOT added here, but worth a look next time someone runs a build: dist/ (27 MB of
# `expo export` WEB output) and .expo/ (local dev state). Both are almost certainly dead weight
# for a native build; left in only because excluding them could not be tested from this session.

# Loose QA/marketing images dropped at the repo root (root-anchored so app assets are safe).
/*.jpeg
/*.jpg
/a1-*.jpeg
/qa-*.jpeg
/art4-final.png
/ob2-role.jpeg

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- SECRETS (never upload)
# These are gitignored, which — per the header — does NOTHING here. They must be named
# explicitly or they ride along in every build tarball. The native build reads none of them:
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are inlined by Metro from the build profile's `env` block in eas.json,
# not from this file, so excluding .env cannot break a build.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
_secrets/
.digest-cron-key.local

# NOT excluded, deliberately — do not "fix" this:
#   ios-certs/ + credentials.json
# eas.json pins `credentialsSource: "local"` on the production profile and points
# submit.production.ios.ascApiKeyPath at ./ios-certs/AuthKey_TNS4WL4GLR.p8. That signing
# material has to reach EAS to sign the build at all — it goes over the credentials channel
# by design — so excluding it here buys zero secrecy and risks breaking production signing.
# The API keys above are different: nothing in the build ever reads them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- build inputs EAS makes itself
# node_modules was NOT here until 2026-09-02, and because this file REPLACES .gitignore (see the
# header) that meant every build uploaded ~600 packages. It is why `eas build` sat for 25 minutes
# on a Windows box with no output and never created a build: the archive step was walking and
# tarring the whole dependency tree.
#
# EAS installs dependencies on the build machine from package.json + the lockfile, so uploading
# them is not merely wasteful, it is wrong — native artifacts built for the wrong platform.
node_modules/

# Generated by `npm run bundle` (the verify gate) and by prebuild/expo. All are rebuilt on the
# build machine, and .git is never needed: EAS gets the source from the archive itself.
.aos-export/
.expo/
dist/
.git/

# Prebuild output. Regenerated from app.json + the config plugins on every build; shipping a stale
# copy would silently override the very config the plugins are supposed to produce.
/ios/
/android/
